From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] lock-util: fall back to POSIX locks without OFD support

unposix_lock() takes its lock with F_OFD_SETLK or F_OFD_SETLKW and has no
fallback. A kernel without open file description locks answers both with
EINVAL, so every caller fails. On the LuneOS Linux 3.4 Halium machines that
includes the /etc/passwd lock, and systemd-sysusers aborts:

  fcntl64(3, F_OFD_SETLKW, {l_type=F_WRLCK, ...}) = -1 EINVAL
  systemd-sysusers: Failed to take /etc/passwd lock: Invalid argument

When an OFD lock is refused with EINVAL, retry the same operation as a plain
POSIX lock. A POSIX lock belongs to the process and not to the file
description, so it is weaker, but it is still a working lock. Kernels that
support OFD locks never take the new path.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [systemd's minimum kernel is much newer than 3.4]
---
--- a/src/basic/lock-util.c
+++ b/src/basic/lock-util.c
@@ -139,6 +139,12 @@
                 .l_len = 0,
         }));
 
+        /* Open file description locks need Linux 3.15. Older kernels refuse F_OFD_SETLK and F_OFD_SETLKW
+         * with EINVAL, so fall back to the plain POSIX lock that unposix_lock() stands in for. It is tied
+         * to the process rather than the file description, which is good enough for the callers here. */
+        if (ofd && r == -EINVAL)
+                return fcntl_lock(fd, operation, /* ofd= */ false);
+
         /* If we are doing non-blocking operations, treat EACCES/EAGAIN the same as per man page. But if
          * not, propagate EACCES back, as it will likely be due to an LSM denying the operation (for example
          * LXC with AppArmor when running on kernel < 6.2), and in some cases we want to gracefully
